# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DayMark — Firebase web configuration
#
# COPY THIS FILE TO `.env.local` AND FILL IN THE VALUES.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Vite only loads `.env`, `.env.local` and `.env.[mode]` files, and it only
# exposes variables that start with `VITE_` to client code.
#
# These values are PUBLIC identifiers that ship inside the browser bundle by
# design — they are not secrets. Real security comes from `firestore.rules`,
# which refuses any document access where `request.auth.uid != userId`.
#
# `.env.local` is gitignored via the `*.local` pattern in `.gitignore`.
#
# IMPORTANT FOR DEPLOYMENT
# Firebase Hosting does not run your build: you run `npm run build` yourself
# and deploy the resulting `dist/` folder. So `.env.local` only has to exist on
# the machine that runs the build. If you ever build in CI (Netlify, Vercel,
# GitHub Actions) you must set these same six variables in that service's
# dashboard — CI will not have your local file.
#
# Get the values from: Firebase Console -> Project settings -> Your apps ->
# SDK setup and configuration -> Config
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
